…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses”„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙAnd Jesus stood before the governor: and the governor asked him, saying, Art thou the King of the Jews? And Jesus said unto him, Thou sayest.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙMAnd when he was accused of the chief priests and elders, he answered nothing.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙVThen said Pilate unto him, Hearest thou not how many things they witness against thee?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙRAnd he answered him to never a word; insomuch that the governor marvelled greatly.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦versesœ„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_Now at that feast the governor was wont to release unto the people a prisoner, whom they would.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ6And they had then a notable prisoner, called Barabbas.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ“Therefore when they were gathered together, Pilate said unto them, Whom will ye that I release unto you? Barabbas, or Jesus which is called Christ?¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ1For he knew that for envy they had delivered him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙºWhen he was set down on the judgment seat, his wife sent unto him, saying, Have thou nothing to do with that just man: for I have suffered many things this day in a dream because of him.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙjBut the chief priests and elders persuaded the multitude that they should ask Barabbas, and destroy Jesus.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtThe governor answered and said unto them, Whether of the twain will ye that I release unto you? They said, Barabbas.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ|Pilate saith unto them, What shall I do then with Jesus which is called Christ? They all say unto him, Let him be crucified.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙnAnd the governor said, Why, what evil hath he done? But they cried out the more, saying, Let him be crucified.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÑWhen Pilate saw that he could prevail nothing, but that rather a tumult was made, he took water, and washed his hands before the multitude, saying, I am innocent of the blood of this just person: see ye to it.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙPThen answered all the people, and said, His blood be on us, and on our children.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfThen released he Barabbas unto them: and when he had scourged Jesus, he delivered him to be crucified.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verseªlast_verse¦verses•„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙtThen the soldiers of the governor took Jesus into the common hall, and gathered unto him the whole band of soldiers.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ5And they stripped him, and put on him a scarlet robe.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ¹And when they had platted a crown of thorns, they put it upon his head, and a reed in his right hand: and they bowed the knee before him, and mocked him, saying, Hail, King of the Jews!¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙEAnd they spit upon him, and took the reed, and smote him on the head.¦layout ªlast_verse„¥verse¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ…And after that they had mocked him, they took the robe off from him, and put his own raiment on him, and led him away to crucify him.¦layout ªlast_verse…¤book'§chapter«first_verse ªlast_verse&¦verses—„¥verse ¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙfAnd as they came out, they found a man of Cyrene, Simon by name: him they compelled to bear his cross.¦layout ªlast_verse „¥verse!¯paragraph_startÃ¥lines‘‚¤textÙYAnd when they were come unto a place called Golgotha, that is to say, a place of a skull,¦layout ªlast_verse!„¥verse"¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙethey gave him vinegar to drink mingled with gall: and when he had tasted thereof, he would not drink.¦layout ªlast_verse"„¥verse#¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙÆAnd they crucified him, and parted his garments, casting lots: that it might be fulfilled which was spoken by the prophet, They parted my garments among them, and upon my vesture did they cast lots.¦layout ªlast_verse#„¥verse$¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ(And sitting down they watched him there;¦layout ªlast_verse$„¥verse%¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙTand set up over his head his accusation written, THIS IS JESUS THE KING OF THE JEWS.¦layout ªlast_verse%„¥verse&¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ_Then were there two thieves crucified with him, one on the right hand, and another on the left.¦layout ªlast_verse&…¤book'§chapter«first_verse'ªlast_verse,¦verses–„¥verse'¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ9And they that passed by reviled him, wagging their heads,¦layout ªlast_verse'„¥verse(¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙand saying, Thou that destroyest the temple, and buildest it in three days, save thyself. If thou be the Son of God, come down from the cross.¦layout ªlast_verse(„¥verse)¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙOLikewise also the chief priests mocking him, with the scribes and elders, said,¦layout ªlast_verse)„¥verse*¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙ„He saved others; himself he cannot save. If he be the King of Israel, let him now come down from the cross, and we will believe him.¦layout ªlast_verse*„¥verse+¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙbHe trusted in God; let him deliver him now, if he will have him: for he said, I am the Son of God.¦layout ªlast_verse+„¥verse,¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙLThe thieves also, which were crucified with him, cast the same in his teeth.¦layout ªlast_verse,…¤book'§chapter«first_verse-ªlast_verse2¦verses–„¥verse-¯paragraph_startÂ¥lines‘‚¤textÙQNow from the 